# Incident Report: INC-2026-10-09-001

---

## 1. Incident Summary

On October 9, 2026 at approximately 18:14 CEST, a level-15 alert (rule 92213 — "Executable file dropped in folder commonly used by malware") was observed on the SOC L1 dashboard originating from WS-CORP-01 (10.10.10.20, VLAN 10 — Corporate). Initial investigation identified an unknown executable (`update.exe`) being written to the user's Downloads directory by a process chain consistent with manual execution. The executable immediately established outbound HTTPS connections to 10.10.66.10 (VLAN 66 — Adversary DMZ), an IP address outside the corporate network.

Subsequent triage revealed a rapid escalation of activity from the same host: system and domain enumeration commands executed via `cmd.exe` spawned by `update.exe`, LSASS process memory access indicative of credential dumping, and SMB administrative share access to DC01 (10.10.10.10, the domain's only Domain Controller). Within minutes, the same executable appeared on DC01 — transferred via the mounted administrative share — followed by a second set of outbound HTTPS connections from DC01 to the same external IP.

The incident was declared at 18:55 CEST after correlation confirmed a multi-phase intrusion: initial execution on a workstation, C2 beaconing, credential theft, lateral movement to the Domain Controller, and domain-level account manipulation. The incident was classified as Critical — the attacker demonstrated the ability to create and delete domain accounts, indicating full compromise of the Active Directory domain.

Containment actions were initiated at 19:00 CEST and completed at 19:30 CEST. Full forensic investigation and domain recovery were escalated to L2 and the domain administration team.

