## 5. Immediate Actions Taken

The following actions were performed by SOC L1 to contain the incident during the shift.

- pfSense firewall rule deployed blocking all outbound traffic from VLAN 10 (Corporate) to 10.10.66.10 (identified C2 destination). Rule verified active via pfSense management interface. This severs the C2 channel without disrupting intra-domain traffic.
- WS-CORP-01 (10.10.10.20) network-isolated via pfSense rule. Only Wazuh agent communication (1514/tcp to 10.10.99.10) was permitted to maintain monitoring visibility during containment. All other traffic blocked.
- DC01 (10.10.10.10) network-isolated via pfSense rule with exceptions for Wazuh agent communication and essential intra-DC services (DNS, Kerberos for existing sessions). Full isolation was deferred to the domain administration team due to the risk of disrupting domain services for other hosts.
- Domain user account `SOCLAB\dbandarica` disabled in Active Directory to prevent further use of the compromised credentials.
- Domain Administrator account password reset initiated in coordination with the domain administration team. The password `Admin!23` — shared between `dbandarica` and Administrator — was confirmed compromised via LSASS dump.
- C2 process (`update.exe`) termination on both hosts was deferred to L2 — killing the process is a containment action, but the forensic team requested it remain running (network-isolated) to preserve the process memory and handle table for analysis.
- Verification performed: `netstat -ano` on both hosts confirmed no active connections to 10.10.66.10 after firewall rules were applied. The C2 beacons continue to attempt check-ins but receive no response.
- Domain account audit requested: domain administration team asked to verify no unknown accounts exist in Active Directory. The `sliver_test` account created and deleted during the attack was confirmed absent, but other persistence mechanisms (scheduled tasks, services, Group Policy modifications) require L2 investigation.
- Incident escalated to SOC L2 with handoff notes attached.

Note: forensic investigation, full domain recovery, credential rotation for all domain accounts, persistence artefact removal, and root cause analysis were escalated to L2 and the domain administration team. These are not part of the L1 scope for this report.

