## 6. Lessons Learned

The following observations were noted during the shift for inclusion in the post-incident review meeting.

- The incident was initially detected through a high-severity built-in rule (92213, level 15) that fired on the executable drop. This was the only alert that appeared prominently on the L1 dashboard without manual searching. The credential dump, lateral movement, and domain account manipulation were all detected by Wazuh but at severity levels (3-6) that did not trigger dashboard notifications or TheHive case creation.
- The MITRE ATT&CK tactics dashboard showed 10 tactics, but the majority of the counts were generated by legitimate Windows operations mapped to attack techniques by built-in rules. Relying on the tactic counts alone would have led to incorrect conclusions about the scope and nature of the attack. The rule descriptions and the process parent-child relationships were the reliable indicators.
- Manual correlation across four data points — the executable drop, the C2 beaconing, the LSASS dump, and the admin share access — was required to reconstruct the attack chain. No automated correlation rule linked these events. Custom detection rules targeting these specific patterns (comsvcs.dll MiniDump, admin share access from non-standard processes, UAC bypass registry writes, domain account creation from workstations) would have provided high-severity, high-fidelity alerts at each phase.
- The time between credential dump (Phase 5) and lateral movement to the Domain Controller (Phase 6) was measured in minutes. The window for intervention between these phases is extremely narrow. Detection at the credential dump stage — before the stolen credentials are used — is the most operationally valuable detection point.
- Password reuse between `dbandarica` and the domain Administrator account was the root cause of the domain compromise. No exploit, vulnerability, or privilege escalation was required to move from the workstation to the Domain Controller. A unique, strong password on the Administrator account would have stopped the attack chain at Phase 5.
- Containment of a Domain Controller is operationally more complex than containment of a workstation. Full network isolation of DC01 would have disrupted domain services for all domain-joined hosts. The partial isolation approach (blocking C2 traffic while preserving essential services) was a pragmatic compromise, but it means the DC remained partially accessible during the containment window.

