## 7. Handoff Notes

The following items are escalated to L2 for follow-up investigation and remediation.

**Forensic investigation required:**
- Full examination of WS-CORP-01 for the LSASS dump file. The dump was written to `C:\Users\Public\lsass.dmp` and reportedly deleted by the attacker after exfiltration, but remnants may exist in unallocated space or volume shadow copies.
- Full examination of DC01 for persistence artefacts: scheduled tasks, services, Group Policy modifications, additional user accounts, startup scripts, and any files dropped beyond `update.exe` at `C:\`.
- Examination of the `update.exe` binary on both hosts: hash verification, static analysis, and identification of the C2 framework (suspected Sliver based on beacon behaviour).
- Review of all domain accounts for unauthorised modifications during the compromise window. The `sliver_test` account was confirmed deleted, but other changes may have been made.
- Review of Kerberos ticket-granting tickets (TGTs) issued during the compromise window. The LSASS dump may have captured Kerberos tickets that could be used for pass-the-ticket attacks even after password rotation.

**Domain recovery required:**
- Immediate password rotation for all domain accounts, starting with the Administrator account and all accounts in Domain Admins, Enterprise Admins, and Schema Admins groups.
- Review and rotation of the KRBTGT account password (double rotation per Microsoft guidance) to invalidate any Golden Tickets that may have been forged.
- Review of Group Policy Objects for unauthorised modifications during the compromise window.
- Re-enable LSA Protection (RunAsPPL) on all domain workstations and servers — investigation should determine whether this was disabled only on WS-CORP-01 or on other hosts as well.
- Enforce unique passwords across privileged accounts — the `Admin!23` password shared between `dbandarica` and Administrator must not recur.

**Detection engineering follow-up:**
- Development and deployment of custom Wazuh detection rules to address the detection gaps observed during this incident. Four rules have been proposed: LSASS dump via comsvcs.dll (100022), SMB admin share access from non-standard process (100023), UAC bypass attempt via ms-settings registry modification (100024), and domain account creation from workstation (100025). Documentation is available under `docs/05-detection-rules/`.
- TheHive webhook integration should be validated to confirm that level ≥ 10 alerts with group `attack` produce automatic case creation. The current incident required manual detection and manual case creation.

**Post-incident review meeting:**
- L1 timeline (this document) contributed as evidence.
- Full root cause analysis, impact assessment, and recommendation prioritisation to be completed by L2, domain administration, and detection engineering leads.
- Review of the MITRE over-mapping problem in Wazuh built-in rules and its impact on analyst decision-making during triage.

